Why this file exists in a private repository
--------------------------------------------

A private repository is not a licensed one. Absent stated terms, a contributor's rights
and the organisation's rights to a contribution are both governed by default copyright
law rather than by anything either party agreed to — which is a poor position to
discover during a dispute rather than before one.

It matters more here than in most repositories, because of ADR-0001. Every repository in
the organisation carries a read-only snapshot of this one at `.paridyum/knowledge/`. That
snapshot is a copy, it is committed, and it travels wherever the repository travels —
including to a contributor's fork under ADR-0012's read-plus-fork model. Terms that apply
to the original need to apply to the copy, and the only way that happens reliably is if
the terms are inside the thing being copied.

Third-party dependencies keep their own licences. `deny.toml` in the service template
enforces an allowlist of permissive licences at build time; nothing here overrides those,
and nothing here applies to them.
